#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128797 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "89f280af-9947-5d54-9284-9ae75d46c682" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of Inertia Given a Description of a Mass Density Function"}] :question/id #uuid "0a99e46a-2104-5285-862b-96b98e7a2f85" :question/math-academy-id "e-12482" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/0cfd94391c0090bc628e4203af3ff15ff7407c9ecc4cc4e7a6f6ac7a1cf36b25.png)\n\nA triangular plate of mass ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/a76c5969032432301de3fc7cda0a904f10aadae5b58e8974db1f29ee18ec14c0.png) occupies the region ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/3c51a5e31ce9f37895e4e38935b015ba8fd40373baa1c8735bd63f3592968af9.png) in the first quadrant enclosed by the lines ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/2997a64fa661559850be321f40a59dd8b408fe6cb662615d51b53cff07ac5a89.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/eb52b396a667b392d8cd2c0913065ec8c9014c014f9284bff1adb3eced294d0c.png) and the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/4d1c3fb0851b2e9ab223c4d204d3554f710a9db8f3442d5869917828d94fe837.png)-axis. The mass density at each point is proportional to its distance from the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/76c6f393964ba1978d27eb9fd4cf2c604384750dba74b6c08e891587e2834a91.png)-axis. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/eafdfda4fc9654c2c5aae57bb072fafa8636c8334ae4b4eed8ff5a7c6076ff4f.png)-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/42913b72caaae9f0740c30760baff88881610a1ced3ff3859222d27d1b8656b4.png) with mass density function ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/3ee7e6579dec5241a245eab4eaf1b1fa3c180e6ec75a557baa9b2497b55558e2.png) we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/84b2f388da9d980669e8b0d6370edb0a12ef82752f16b4fb1b6dc6ee4c44c03d.png)-axis is given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/60a59f8ad0379b033d9d2bac9d67afe5c40a43f6d1f2bb6169b90ca1a78221aa.png)\n\n- The moment of inertia about the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/00ad0bc1dadfb43a63229d80b11e02988bbe482c5d5122ff28c54bc3d2309231.png)-axis is given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/b36595e561b6dadab98447cbca0621828e651a2a3616504357aa90d13a7a20b2.png)\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/4cfac7a7f94c2c55d261fd1e84810bf193920e1003cc0fdc6e841fcb8d8b3f1d.png)\n\nFirst, notice that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/05ff793b0aee1f05a79216f9e7f119afa544749c81077937f8f06a353f6ef4eb.png) is a type I region with representation\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/e4896170bd06ee1f66dd595e504209977df6611d8c5c4fa0cbf28d2dc146beaf.png)\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/c1301c17b1dbb8b48716d09ad6f0a7a105917ea7a1c0e942bbe087b12772f254.png)\n\nFrom the description, the mass density function can be written as \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/3bc1fabeed9c38d36d49380c17ba47c6c0a9d6a1723bed3a4f6e33467bd893e2.png)\nwhere ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/c26b2d7388707b35315aac38bf33a36573a637e3410d1a8e2797fc4164f6d438.png) is a constant of proportionality. \n\nSince the density contains the unknown constant ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/d61c1c56d7c970921acb23214ab2ece4bbd33fefbf212b481ca95fd65fb926df.png), we first use the total mass ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/86f38d0431d36a6b7010ff7611af19c2c54116e9465f15e5a36cf85c933e3054.png) to determine ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/9025c6a94271bd94be0bb930ab14cdb7611034b480b7b56ca76767f957f789c7.png)\n\nWe can find an expression for ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/e375b825a2d61d973c964706a6bf27c9af8a19b3594f02cdf70afb62b988e66b.png) in terms of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/6e32bbbc4bffbd45f252c9185c7760901b027dd554ef49d044c78e87331029b4.png) by integrating the density function over the plate:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/e4fc94647cb930e64247e9bac85885f6ac4fae1ed132be30f35db0f3723f4f43.png)\n\nTherefore, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/399765a98ec95ac5bc4deb478463b2787bfa1d6a17a2ea0c9d6f026dd6b79563.png) and hence ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/f1e138092994505279c36db8afdf2e7df102c4efbdb5803515077b076009bf81.png) Therefore, the moment of inertia about the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/00ad0bc1dadfb43a63229d80b11e02988bbe482c5d5122ff28c54bc3d2309231.png)-axis is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/3a0e13d8b9068b72cc88b446cfa2fa0c3ac418b9001013a0f630cb5d621b3f6a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128798 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eba1f811-7b7d-56f6-8b49-4e7a4c7db81b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Moments of Inertia Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/id #uuid "dbf6a3b7-abb9-5f62-83af-00169c673bc6" :question/math-academy-id "e-12483" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/12fb5f07885b074f983cab3a5fba0faff607d8aa34d7cd3abe4e94abf53dac25.png)\n\nConsider the plate that occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the polar circles $r = 1$ and $r = 2$ and the polar rays $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ and $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ in the first quadrant, as shown above. The mass density function of the plate is given by $\\lambda(x,y) = \\dfrac{1}{x^2}.$ Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda(r,\\theta),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\nI_x = \\iint\\limits_D \\lambda(r,\\theta) \\cdot r^3\\sin^2{\\theta} \\:\\textrm{d}r\\:\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\nI_y = \\iint\\limits_D \\lambda(r,\\theta) \\cdot  r^3\\cos^2{\\theta} \\:\\textrm{d}r\\:\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\nI_0 = \\iint\\limits_D \\lambda(r,\\theta) \\cdot  r^3 \\:\\textrm{d}r\\:\\textrm{d}\\theta = I_x + I_y.\n$$\n\nFirst, note that the region $D$ in polar coordinates can be expressed as\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 1 \\leq r \\leq 2, \\:\\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nAlso, the mass density function in polar coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\lambda(r,\\theta) = \\dfrac{1}{r^2 \\cos^2{\\theta}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{1}^{2}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =({\\int }_{1}^{2}r\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4}\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}\\,{|}_{1}^{2}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4-1)\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128965 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "92cc3d27-0e31-571f-9b92-4f209c5a08e0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of Inertia of a Lamina With Uniform Mass Density"}] :question/id #uuid "c317696a-899d-5af5-9bf4-2d38862f5877" :question/math-academy-id "e-8542" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/de882e240d0a6ca824e77397fe9c6b51eb94d36c2e5e3524422ef0a0f28103de.png)\n\nA triangular plate of mass ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/48c72d7f57bb7f3163fa37903a032773c12ddce6fdc0e0bee8818259aba9e465.png) and uniform mass density occupies the region ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/05ff793b0aee1f05a79216f9e7f119afa544749c81077937f8f06a353f6ef4eb.png) enclosed between the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/54d73239bada862f9b8d6d3d7a4f73bea15432668f6b2116098faa02a4b3d435.png)-axis and the lines ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/3879c9475dcb86235e269108d73767f2e631eee68e65aacbfffbc22ada2033e1.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/52ab6e6677e9404e8b08bfff7b90cbfb332b344d4fe8ecc19b603ef05616ebf7.png) as shown above. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/f0a3d06d2c55c6889a22a1cc4e11adb35b71e24e1395e6d6048c1135620c225f.png)-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/28390f00cdb34a228868c36035ff66f1ea65bf7ff5e592fa473fca18d6addbcc.png) with mass density function ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/c97ba2315cf57f0ca52513a553203387b121396b6b0a5c96fc9a67e0ae6798d4.png) we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/245bebfeb6cb95babe872c0185e33d598fb9dd08c8950252c07cdb5f5c8f87d4.png)-axis is given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/b8379c5ab1146e79464ee5677bc05edac3c6a9368629746b08c9a9e66109681c.png)\n\n- The moment of inertia about the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/4cf2e90080f1ae459e72ac253d6617d0defdcc3be58a1ca385bddc3215034eca.png)-axis is given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/ac1949df7033d3a968286404f658fea6ad7de10f446d6ba8f4090bf40fa26afa.png)\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/509782164a1c349c9ba8b6399b252decae9b022ffde15d840bc68b123872071d.png)\n\nFirst, notice that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/3c51a5e31ce9f37895e4e38935b015ba8fd40373baa1c8735bd63f3592968af9.png) is a type I region with representation\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/e666ae60e603116812051833c70d32199e3f146e23fc99260c274fb546bfeef8.png)\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/68904cbee1839a61f4d65c5ff03066d8f28b8a5e070f6ddb00c82c29cfd93fcd.png)\n\nThe area of the triangular plate ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/eebd5724c98ea00681ef901dabae1d0b3428aff0c6e6a9cdadc020ed46f129c7.png) is \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/0285f4ab3e7a9c974f244d0ab517ca737d2a5aec35bfbceb779037d54ce00f98.png)\n\nSince the plate has uniform density, every unit of area contains the same amount of mass. Therefore, the mass density is constant and is given by the total mass divided by the total area:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/d3089c43ce61bf64a57a6d64b38987b91a6c8f9aa9f06d106f4c124ed430dd09.png)\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/235efd30dbd8ca8883230793c8b79882afd19443c612b54f30b5cd507a646c9c.png)-axis is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/148b58f76868ac2f7a85e68627f91f2fa47c6c6a2bd5bcb67af8ba8af011167a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128966 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fdc1a2a-8a35-59f2-9890-5b835693df4f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of Inertia of a Lamina Given a Mass Density Function"}] :question/id #uuid "bee275c6-cca6-515f-b737-597ecca278a6" :question/math-academy-id "e-8543" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/46d6a57700d1df7f2de9a46507821a8553876eb956be6ef87a455d5ca0fabafb.png)\n\nA plate occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the curves $y=x^2$ and $y=1$ in the first quadrant, as shown above. If the mass density function of $D$ is given by $\\lambda(x,y) = 4xy,$ find the moment of inertia of the lamina about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda(x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\nI_x =  \\iint\\limits_D y^2\\lambda(x,y)  \\:\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\nI_y =  \\iint\\limits_D x^2\\lambda(x,y)  \\:\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\nI_0 = \\iint\\limits_D (x^2+y^2) \\lambda(x,y)  \\:\\textrm{d}A = I_x + I_y.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: x^2 \\leq y \\leq 1 \\right\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/8846da0fe3b1e8369a9277bbaba1e082d040136a71105a5816af7b13d2788027.png)\n\nUsing the formula for $I_x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}{y}^{2}\\cdot 4xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}4x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[x{y}^{4}]_{y={x}^{2}}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(1-{x}^{8})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x-{x}^{9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{10}}{10}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129466 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "92cc3d27-0e31-571f-9b92-4f209c5a08e0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of Inertia of a Lamina With Uniform Mass Density"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63d942ae-7ca7-5f90-8642-65c757b1b8a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{M}{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364351} {:answer/id #uuid "92171c15-efca-5856-a07d-979bbc4af5e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5M}{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364352} {:answer/id #uuid "fd81b4e7-ffb1-58eb-a458-95b4181c57bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2M\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364353} {:answer/id #uuid "7fc56543-759b-54d0-a7fd-5e7d8021187a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2M}{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364354} {:answer/id #uuid "b3428d5f-9137-5d72-a1e6-aede2246c334" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4M}{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364355}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92171c15-efca-5856-a07d-979bbc4af5e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5M}{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364352} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c9e89ea-a32a-5f42-b5df-2f12d0b16131" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364350}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d5a597a5-f786-5891-91b3-c78b73413f55" :question/math-academy-id "q-162905" :question/problem "A rectangular plate of mass $M\\,\\text{kg}$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2},$ where $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the origin." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $A=1\\cdot 2=2.$ \n\nSince the plate has uniform density, every unit of area contains the same amount of mass. Therefore, the mass density is constant and is given by the total mass divided by the total area:\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for ${I}_{0},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{3}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}{[\\frac{1}{3}y+\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}(\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{8}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}\\cdot \\frac{10}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5M}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia is $\\frac{5M}{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129468 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fdc1a2a-8a35-59f2-9890-5b835693df4f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of Inertia of a Lamina Given a Mass Density Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d781213d-3507-5e5a-88ff-0ad3250f0965" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "102.5\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364357} {:answer/id #uuid "e467db90-28a4-5ff2-8947-d45bcaf68497" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "87.5\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364358} {:answer/id #uuid "e825da35-c780-562e-b498-1fa445f91ce4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "97.5\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364359} {:answer/id #uuid "1cf76319-8912-5c67-a20a-2fba906dc284" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364360} {:answer/id #uuid "55acb80b-9f02-5dde-81b6-624097d9e308" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "95\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364361}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e825da35-c780-562e-b498-1fa445f91ce4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "97.5\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364359} :answer-field/id #uuid "4cd5dda2-9253-53d3-b8a6-2121b03fe510" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364356}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e40dca43-d21c-5ed7-bbc5-5f37b500695b" :question/math-academy-id "q-162922" :question/problem "A rectangular plate occupies the region\n$D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 3}$\nwith mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=(6-xy)\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2},$ where $x$ and $y$ area measured in meters. Find the moment of inertia of this lamina about the origin." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for ${I}_{0},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})(6-xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}6{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}y+6{y}^{2}-x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{[2{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}y+6x{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}16-4y+12{y}^{2}-2{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[16y-2{y}^{2}+4{y}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{4}]_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =48-18+108-\\frac{81}{2}-0 \\\\  & =97.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia of the lamina about the origin is $97.5\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129470 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "89f280af-9947-5d54-9284-9ae75d46c682" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of Inertia Given a Description of a Mass Density Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "889e1bde-98ec-5520-81d3-bfc61b369742" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18M}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126627} {:answer/id #uuid "90131c7e-7376-577f-baa2-1714df60568d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15M}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126628} {:answer/id #uuid "cc2842e7-e4e9-5230-b246-d1bd1565af15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16M}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126629} {:answer/id #uuid "13e80e32-a0e9-5331-a0bb-e614d0752b91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16M}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126630} {:answer/id #uuid "342c2a96-e444-508c-aa2c-b9df291a1df2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{20M}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126631}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "889e1bde-98ec-5520-81d3-bfc61b369742" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18M}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126627} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a8c9c55-6640-5a69-8e00-97608a2660ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126626}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "54c0ec35-7150-5d58-acd5-421129a7e7ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-162986" :question/problem "A rectangular plate of mass $M$ occupies the region\n$D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 3}.$\nThe mass density at each point is proportional to the square of its distance from the $y$-axis.\nFind the moment of inertia of the lamina about the origin." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFrom the description, the mass density function can be written as $\\lambda (x,y)=k{x}^{2},$ where $k$ is a constant of proportionality. We can find an expression for $k$ in terms of $M$ by integrating the density function over the plate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}k{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =k({\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x)({\\int }_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =k{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{1}\\cdot [y]_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =k(\\frac{1}{3}-0)(3-0) \\\\  & =k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=M$ and hence $\\lambda (x,y)=k{x}^{2}=M{x}^{2}.$\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =M{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =M{\\int }_{0}^{3}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{4}+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & =M{\\int }_{0}^{3}{[\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}{y}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =M{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =M{[\\frac{1}{5}y+\\frac{1}{9}{y}^{3}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =M(\\frac{3}{5}+3-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{18M}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129471 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "89f280af-9947-5d54-9284-9ae75d46c682" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of Inertia Given a Description of a Mass Density Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6d95c32-d6b0-52f0-9e18-ded300260902" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7M}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126633} {:answer/id #uuid "ebe2fd3e-425d-5278-a8af-ccfe6d086112" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3M}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126634} {:answer/id #uuid "f188ff5e-3ac1-535a-9ca9-d8daf20bc68d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8M}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126635} {:answer/id #uuid "2d031545-04fb-56a1-94d8-4f128a3345dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4M}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126636} {:answer/id #uuid "7b230073-2983-5000-a28d-28363f80c07f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{M}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126637}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d031545-04fb-56a1-94d8-4f128a3345dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4M}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126636} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f7e6a23-873d-56aa-b92e-49a46d29281e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126632}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0584f4c5-c47d-5f81-8ff4-5bc6e28a681f" :question/math-academy-id "q-162987" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/c290abf6acdff985b9c4aae6a998d7607b251a28285c9a8c15ff7c672e4ddc59.png)\n\nA triangular plate of mass $M$ occupies the region\n$D$ in the first quadrant enclosed by the line $y=2-x$ and the coordinate axes. The mass density at each point is proportional to the sum of its distance from the $x$-axis and its distance from the $y$-axis. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2-x},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/50ee44f265b3b27d7751e47a142053e06ee444b42481cd1277b5dacbb3ba8583.png)\n\nFrom the description, the mass density function can be written as \n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=k(|x|+|y|)=k(x+y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant of proportionality. We can find an expression for $k$ in terms of $M$ by integrating the density function over the plate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2-x}k(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2-x}x+y\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[xy+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=2-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{2}x(2-x)+\\frac{1}{2}(2-x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{2}2-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{[2x-\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =k(4-\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8k}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=\\frac{3M}{8}$ and hence, \n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=k(x+y)=\\frac{3M}{8}(x+y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2-x}{y}^{2}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2-x}x{y}^{2}+{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{3}x{y}^{3}+\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{4}]}_{y=0}^{y=2-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{3}x(2-x)^{3}+\\frac{1}{4}(2-x)^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{12}(2-x)^{3}(x+6)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{32}{\\int }_{0}^{2}-{x}^{4}+24{x}^{2}-64x+48\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{32}{[-\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}+8{x}^{3}-32{x}^{2}+48x]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{32}(-\\frac{32}{5}+64-128+96) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{32}\\cdot \\frac{128}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364362 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fdc1a2a-8a35-59f2-9890-5b835693df4f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of Inertia of a Lamina Given a Mass Density Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66686c14-240c-5499-994c-2e573bd1e5db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364364} {:answer/id #uuid "59e83f3b-c47f-5e82-87f4-ba33cf0a2b66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364365} {:answer/id #uuid "d04c5e36-7464-5e87-89a2-8006f03b7e00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364366} {:answer/id #uuid "8037ba45-c4ae-5f45-9a1f-0cc83372148b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364367} {:answer/id #uuid "91f9b625-df19-5f5e-a030-ee60b051098e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364368}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91f9b625-df19-5f5e-a030-ee60b051098e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364368} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc0c365a-dbfe-57ac-a777-1d559f1db0e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364363}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "567c1d0c-a247-52c4-bf76-77c7361081f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-163146" :question/problem "A rectangular plate occupies the region\n$D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 3}$\nwith mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=(x+y)\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2},$ where $x$ and $y$ area measured in meters. Find the moment of inertia of this lamina about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for ${I}_{y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}y]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}4+\\frac{8y}{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[4y+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{3}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =12+12 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia of the lamina about the $y$-axis is $24\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364369 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fdc1a2a-8a35-59f2-9890-5b835693df4f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of Inertia of a Lamina Given a Mass Density Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d26df32d-76b1-51df-9db9-495b9ec94532" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364371} {:answer/id #uuid "5da41ef1-88a5-535c-b30e-1ff260fbac75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23.5\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364372} {:answer/id #uuid "40de387c-1935-5be6-ab94-1f06ef117d79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30.1\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364373} {:answer/id #uuid "d2a4a770-a674-56d5-bb46-bedb77f6cde4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32.4\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364374} {:answer/id #uuid "0f23d03c-005e-545c-96a4-ed25a92c7cd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364375}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f23d03c-005e-545c-96a4-ed25a92c7cd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364375} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcf48675-2df6-583b-ae44-9be89f0aa298" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364370}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bc886bf2-5eb8-54a6-890d-ce84fe739bf9" :question/math-academy-id "q-163170" :question/problem "A rectangular plate occupies the region\n$D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}$\nwith mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=(4y+1)\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2},$ where $x$ and $y$ area measured in meters. Find the moment of inertia of this lamina about the origin." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for ${I}_{0},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})(4y+1)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{3}4{x}^{2}y+{x}^{2}+4{y}^{3}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{3}y+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+4x{y}^{3}+x{y}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}36y+9+12{y}^{3}+3{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[18{y}^{2}+9y+3{y}^{4}+{y}^{3}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =18+9+3+1 \\\\  & =31.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia of the lamina about the origin is $31\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364376 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fdc1a2a-8a35-59f2-9890-5b835693df4f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of Inertia of a Lamina Given a Mass Density Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce215896-0628-504b-b6c2-51ecc21f5b3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "66" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364378} {:answer/id #uuid "1676c11d-0acd-5884-b7e3-089cf89e933b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "88" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364379} {:answer/id #uuid "09c0fa76-d163-5cda-92bb-73991ff8b828" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "112" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364380} {:answer/id #uuid "b579d07c-e4eb-5d46-8f45-12a7c3432faa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364381} {:answer/id #uuid "1bb18832-4b39-594d-b940-8af02a8390d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "124" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364382}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09c0fa76-d163-5cda-92bb-73991ff8b828" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "112" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364380} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab77f835-d061-575b-91fa-5ebec5fb210e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364377}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fcf4074c-6e0d-5c3f-8497-b2b3a7532e5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-163174" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/a6910defd242e1b65b7bdf8b580cd2f56df3ac43951a9f83f353003e00a849b4.png)\n\nA plate occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the lines $y=0$, $x=4$, and the curve $y=\\sqrt{x}$, as shown above. If the mass density function of $D$ is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=3y,$ find the moment of inertia of the lamina about the origin." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 4,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x}},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/dd5ee021dd73317a775c0ea653212f7ee5545f8c6854e6a9daabdc93b5c3b9c0.png)\n\nUsing the formula for ${I}_{0},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot 3y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}3{x}^{2}y+3{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{[\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}{y}^{4}]}_{y=0}^{y=\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{3}+\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3}{8}{x}^{4}+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =96+16 \\\\  & =112.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364383 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "89f280af-9947-5d54-9284-9ae75d46c682" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of Inertia Given a Description of a Mass Density Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6aa7e02-f624-574e-bb4e-88134b2affc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{M}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364385} {:answer/id #uuid "5a0b84bc-628a-5173-906c-44830a8aee5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364386} {:answer/id #uuid "0b399939-864d-5411-99c0-09e69ee0ac82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364387} {:answer/id #uuid "3e4fd8ae-55f7-50aa-932b-003440a00ce7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364388} {:answer/id #uuid "73c1e694-e7d4-5518-ab83-8afc20afe7d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{M}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364389}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b399939-864d-5411-99c0-09e69ee0ac82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364387} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb2c7b68-a5b3-5920-aa62-d8a781ac907a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364384}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a8bb967c-3713-5f4d-a731-98f880cc76f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-163180" :question/problem "A rectangular plate of mass $M$ occupies the region\n$D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}.$\nThe mass density at each point is proportional to its distance from the $x$-axis.\nFind the moment of inertia of the lamina about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFrom the description, the mass density function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=k|y|=ky,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant of proportionality. We can find an expression for $k$ in terms of $M$ by integrating the density function over the plate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}ky\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =k({\\int }_{0}^{3}\\text{d}x)({\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =k[x]_{0}^{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =k(3-0)(\\frac{1}{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3k}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=\\frac{2M}{3}$ and hence,\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=ky=\\frac{2M}{3}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{2}\\cdot y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{3}({\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x)({\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{3}(9-0)(\\frac{1}{2}-0) \\\\  & =3M.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364390 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fdc1a2a-8a35-59f2-9890-5b835693df4f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of Inertia of a Lamina Given a Mass Density Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7dabfa54-9e3c-58d8-82f8-1c95511706dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364392} {:answer/id #uuid "8e90fbcf-fcbb-5e76-a8d1-5677776f1a48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364393} {:answer/id #uuid "0d7055ee-c8cf-540a-a1bc-012444b8e4be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "58" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364394} {:answer/id #uuid "cae28922-3f15-53ec-a4df-1e27139d3f43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364395} {:answer/id #uuid "1b495bc3-cfae-516f-a9db-c89e4c622cf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364396}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7dabfa54-9e3c-58d8-82f8-1c95511706dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364392} :answer-field/id #uuid "51723526-c4a3-5890-b64c-fee18c254140" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364391}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "65252fa1-bbed-5575-8f72-fbaa2782faa7" :question/math-academy-id "q-163183" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/dc61efd6e5ef1fd46fca53f7c1e47394c6b9c95fb2455dc0c798d9936a68631b.png)\n\nA plate occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the $x$-axis, $y$-axis, and the curve $y=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}$ in the first quadrant, as shown above. If the mass density function of $D$ is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=10y,$ find the moment of inertia of the lamina about the origin." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/626cbf8e182d9eebb8b48026f07c5dd012c1096cc9568465f3648710ea0d07a4.png)\n\nUsing the formula for ${I}_{0},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})(10y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}10{x}^{2}y+10{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[5{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}{y}^{4}]}_{y=0}^{y=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}5{x}^{2}(4-{x}^{2})+\\frac{5}{2}{(4-{x}^{2})}^{2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{5}{2}(4-{x}^{2})(2{x}^{2}+4-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}16-{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{[16x-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}(32-\\frac{32}{5}) \\\\  & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364397 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "89f280af-9947-5d54-9284-9ae75d46c682" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of Inertia Given a Description of a Mass Density Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "248aa271-9fed-56db-aa8f-33e3edd221ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5M}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364399} {:answer/id #uuid "7b915a04-7d07-5279-a976-5d8ed6f3a5dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{M}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364400} {:answer/id #uuid "f66ff212-5369-5e1e-b590-da1a09692154" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4M}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364401} {:answer/id #uuid "1ec3ff84-9b57-52f9-83f2-5b24743886d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2M}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364402} {:answer/id #uuid "16083e51-11b0-5a19-982b-a26975d3c199" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7M}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364403}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ec3ff84-9b57-52f9-83f2-5b24743886d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2M}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364402} :answer-field/id #uuid "70b3f9a6-e0ce-518d-a889-3f10144e1a0f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364398}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "62c73ee4-534d-5429-b48c-9e2202076205" :question/math-academy-id "q-163186" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/043119a0637985fa55a7084a8d4b67df5031b24211ab421ec019700235ca2365.png)\n\nA triangular plate of mass $M$ occupies the region $D$ in the first quadrant enclosed by the line $y=1-x$ and the coordinate axes. The mass density at each point is proportional to the square of its distance from the $x$-axis. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1-x},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/782b4df0220d84d90d26a4ad1fd4a34da00ecf3c5d62a9416d9f0490ca765e2b.png)\n\nFrom the description, the mass density function can be written as $\\lambda (x,y)=k{y}^{2},$ where $k$ is a constant of proportionality. We can find an expression for $k$ in terms of $M$ by integrating the density function over the plate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}k{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{0}^{1-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{3}(1-x)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{3}{[-\\frac{1}{4}(1-x)^{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=12M$ and hence $\\lambda (x,y)=k{y}^{2}=12M{y}^{2}.$ Therefore, the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =12M{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}{y}^{2}({y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =12M{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}{y}^{4}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =12M{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{5}{y}^{5}]}_{0}^{1-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =12M{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{5}(1-x)^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{12M}{5}{[-\\frac{1}{6}(1-x)^{6}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{12M}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364404 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "89f280af-9947-5d54-9284-9ae75d46c682" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of Inertia Given a Description of a Mass Density Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02e6630c-651d-5a2c-b421-84ec3f775f36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364406} {:answer/id #uuid "2f79fbf5-ceb2-58cb-b025-5d89e2b58594" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364407} {:answer/id #uuid "e9883c73-3b5e-5e84-a8d6-41d516bb16a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8M}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364408} {:answer/id #uuid "1a6dec5d-8904-5e76-b332-526199c33536" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7M}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364409} {:answer/id #uuid "69cf6182-90b0-56b9-8e61-b2bf21564f81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2M}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364410}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9883c73-3b5e-5e84-a8d6-41d516bb16a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8M}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364408} :answer-field/id #uuid "55f4bfc2-50a7-54e6-af10-d51346698c9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364405}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "474a3fe6-26c2-5d5a-8503-ddb09ee16592" :question/math-academy-id "q-163189" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/37fde9d99a144ab65874a9fa329bc9a4ca32b0f4a6981993134e98c47a32e07f.png)\n\nA triangular plate of mass $M$ occupies the region $D$ in the first quadrant enclosed by the lines $y=x,$ $x=2,$ and the $x$-axis. The mass density at each point is proportional to the square of its distance from the $y$-axis. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le x},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/f08ac68e3d8992e70cdc40eddfb9cdc39ee16a275890f3d9d7db42a682866bf6.png)\n\nFrom the description, the mass density function can be written as $\\lambda (x,y)=k{x}^{2},$ where $k$ is a constant of proportionality. We can find an expression for $k$ in terms of $M$ by integrating the density function over the plate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}k{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[{x}^{2}y]}_{y=0}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}(x-0)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =4k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=\\frac{M}{4}$ and hence,\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=k{x}^{2}=\\frac{M}{4}{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{x}^{2}({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[{x}^{4}y]}_{y=0}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{4}(x-0)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{4}{[\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{6}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{4}\\cdot \\frac{32}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8M}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364411 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "92cc3d27-0e31-571f-9b92-4f209c5a08e0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of Inertia of a Lamina With Uniform Mass Density"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ee795c3-412f-5107-9cb9-1762c7cb3946" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12M\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364413} {:answer/id #uuid "726114c7-bbe3-5a44-81ca-b02d8db567a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364414} {:answer/id #uuid "2cdda060-9202-592a-92bf-841692f3c5d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6M\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364415} {:answer/id #uuid "c22ca50d-9e77-58a5-9164-d2bd4133b2ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10M\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364416} {:answer/id #uuid "ac2fa324-8b6c-5fce-af77-b1abb4b29b7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8M}{9}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364417}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c22ca50d-9e77-58a5-9164-d2bd4133b2ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10M\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364416} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a61c7d5-8b5c-5b7f-a488-5712f640356b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364412}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bdbe5d34-53d8-5fa8-ab86-b773c4a96924" :question/math-academy-id "q-163200" :question/problem "A rectangular plate of mass $M\\,\\text{kg}$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\,1\\le y\\le 4},$ where $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the origin." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $A=3\\cdot 3=9.$ \n\nSince the plate has uniform density, every unit of area contains the same amount of mass. Therefore, the mass density is constant and is given by the total mass divided by the total area:\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{9}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for ${I}_{0},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{9}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{9}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{9}{\\int }_{1}^{4}9+3{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{9}{[9y+{y}^{3}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{9}(36+64-9-1) \\\\  & =10M\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia is $10M\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364418 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "92cc3d27-0e31-571f-9b92-4f209c5a08e0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of Inertia of a Lamina With Uniform Mass Density"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "015aa5d4-82bd-5776-aee9-772d09b74aa3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8M\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364420} {:answer/id #uuid "70123a16-b5fa-58d6-b8ba-b239f81826f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4M\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364421} {:answer/id #uuid "41efbba0-2e82-554e-985c-edd9c3305f7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16M}{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364422} {:answer/id #uuid "d75264c0-fda0-5330-b7a1-f956fd948186" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2M}{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364423} {:answer/id #uuid "1ad647f5-560d-5d2d-b6db-c8030bd75321" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4M}{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364424}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ad647f5-560d-5d2d-b6db-c8030bd75321" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4M}{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364424} :answer-field/id #uuid "c67bffe3-ab54-5774-84cd-532a1173c729" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364419}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "79ad7a44-69b7-56af-9f4d-f253dc7ebf95" :question/math-academy-id "q-163204" :question/problem "A rectangular plate of mass $M\\,\\text{kg}$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,1\\le y\\le 3},$ where $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $A=2\\cdot 2=4.$ \n\nSince the plate has uniform density, every unit of area contains the same amount of mass. Therefore, the mass density is constant and is given by the total mass divided by the total area:\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{4}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for ${I}_{y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{8}{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{4}{[\\frac{8y}{3}]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{3}(3-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia is $\\frac{4M}{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364425 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "92cc3d27-0e31-571f-9b92-4f209c5a08e0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of Inertia of a Lamina With Uniform Mass Density"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cbb20b5a-b47c-5eb6-81c2-91d3970ea90c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{M}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364427} {:answer/id #uuid "413ef5e7-06b3-55df-a5f0-694083b4c0f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3M}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364428} {:answer/id #uuid "f47f5895-e52d-5cb1-a5a4-2aae43678d8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364429} {:answer/id #uuid "d3df9fb8-d934-55c9-bb4b-7236aaa66940" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364430} {:answer/id #uuid "aa3544b6-e032-526f-a164-ef74ea80aabf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{M}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364431}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "413ef5e7-06b3-55df-a5f0-694083b4c0f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3M}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364428} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d7449fa-6802-5070-99f2-2c2cd1437003" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364426}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "79941e95-b786-504a-89df-1f073b7dd438" :question/math-academy-id "q-163217" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/de3753a45554be28283e74116510ff6c3b67560fce79d1570c819a215f6e10f3.png)\n\nA triangular plate of mass $M$ and uniform mass density occupies the region $D$ enclosed between the $x$-axis and the lines $y=3x$ and $y=4-x,$ as shown above. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type II region with representation\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 3,\\,\\,\\frac{y}{3}\\le x\\le 4-y},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/47c897bd58a23965d9c36f683553c0817afa8bc2e37ed53a432edcd62b386e55.png)\n\nThe area of the triangular plate $D$ is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 3=6.\n$$\n\nSince the plate has uniform density, every unit of area contains the same amount of mass. Therefore, the mass density is constant and is given by the total mass divided by the total area:\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{y/3}^{4-y}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{y}^{2}[{\\int }_{y/3}^{4-y}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{y}^{2}(4-y-\\frac{1}{3}y)\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}4{y}^{2}-\\frac{4}{3}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{[\\frac{4}{3}{y}^{3}-\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{4}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}(36-27) \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364432 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "92cc3d27-0e31-571f-9b92-4f209c5a08e0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of Inertia of a Lamina With Uniform Mass Density"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "190d8080-e400-57a6-b5b8-8afabda13574" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3M}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364434} {:answer/id #uuid "b73bdc1f-af7c-5714-bddb-c368e2f4687a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2M}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364435} {:answer/id #uuid "6d2b9ca3-bc1a-5ff9-adc7-fce2197d707a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364436} {:answer/id #uuid "da917780-39cd-5928-9fdc-5504eb35b69b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364437} {:answer/id #uuid "b32ba64b-9d31-5c48-b895-5c66576bad5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{M}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364438}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b73bdc1f-af7c-5714-bddb-c368e2f4687a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2M}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364435} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e76fa7e-92b9-5529-9a53-a0882995a30b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364433}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "241848f8-5ad9-56aa-808b-8b4d0f7ad9b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-163219" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/c683f411b11b7b353e166364c8e0fdbc0d218c07535c2f3430d832ab129702d8.png)\n\nA triangular plate of mass $M$ and uniform mass density occupies the region $D$ enclosed between the $y$-axis and the lines $y=\\frac{x}{2}$ and $y=3-x,$ as shown above. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,\\frac{x}{2}\\le y\\le 3-x},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/6c2cec5e919c25623396b5ba5f052481623a868c7520aa8e3d1d3eb34f3a4a58.png)\n\nThe area of the triangular plate $D$ is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 3=3.\n$$\n\nSince the plate has uniform density, every unit of area contains the same amount of mass. Therefore, the mass density is constant and is given by the total mass divided by the total area:\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{x/2}^{3-x}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}[{\\int }_{x/2}^{3-x}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}(3-x-\\frac{x}{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3{x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{[{x}^{3}-\\frac{3}{8}{x}^{4}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}(8-6) \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364439 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eba1f811-7b7d-56f6-8b49-4e7a4c7db81b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Moments of Inertia Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "148b67cd-5e6d-5122-8b85-df375fc39a1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364441} {:answer/id #uuid "1a18306e-b769-5961-943d-ed487a9c6ee5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364442} {:answer/id #uuid "123883c4-49ea-51f6-8d86-af36715fefa9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364443} {:answer/id #uuid "6ea984eb-8ee9-50ba-a64c-c051cb0ff9cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364444} {:answer/id #uuid "23e32cb4-858f-5260-ada4-11aef88bd8c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364445}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "148b67cd-5e6d-5122-8b85-df375fc39a1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364441} :answer-field/id #uuid "1675b19d-6a25-51b5-b3d7-0c1339c6f8fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364440}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "92176aef-29a6-58f5-b012-4b4737ad5698" :question/math-academy-id "q-163225" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/d1ef5062d5bc045c1e1f7fa58c513315852f74aa15539c001662b0055583f01b.png)\n\nConsider the plate that occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the polar circles $r=1$ and $r=2$ and the polar rays $\\theta =0$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ in the first quadrant, as shown above. The mass density function of the plate is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=10x.$ Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the origin." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (r,\\theta ),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, note that the region $D$ in polar coordinates can be expressed as\n\n$$\nD={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}}.\n$$\n\nAlso, the mass density function in polar coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\lambda (r,\\theta )=10r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{1}^{2}10r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{1}^{2}10{r}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =({\\int }_{1}^{2}10{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =2{r}^{5}\\,{|}_{1}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =2(32-1)(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-0) \\\\  & =31\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364446 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eba1f811-7b7d-56f6-8b49-4e7a4c7db81b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Moments of Inertia Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0235e39f-4899-563f-b750-9b6144eae62c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364448} {:answer/id #uuid "10549c83-7c09-5681-addb-db839d171f43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364449} {:answer/id #uuid "aba3ea2a-4d17-5950-95f9-b3cf3aa534e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364450} {:answer/id #uuid "6e6e0742-2720-5afe-811c-3dbf85e34cc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364451} {:answer/id #uuid "d5ac7b28-da9d-59db-9a14-f95a0e52500f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364452}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5ac7b28-da9d-59db-9a14-f95a0e52500f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364452} :answer-field/id #uuid "69501c94-9cc9-56b3-80cd-aa847b5d3a43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364447}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5d537522-22cf-5837-a35b-5a4b7b536424" :question/math-academy-id "q-163228" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/044395de3b30755d0c83d489709c38420827f6984af740bc56a3dc5bfaa33794.png)\n\nConsider the plate that occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the polar circles $r=2$ and $r=3$ and the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ in the first quadrant, as shown above. The mass density function of the plate is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}.$ Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (r,\\theta ),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, note that the region $D$ in polar coordinates can be expressed as\n\n$$\nD={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,2\\le r\\le 3,\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nAlso, the mass density function in polar coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{1}{{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{2}^{3}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =({\\int }_{2}^{3}r\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}\\,{|}_{2}^{3}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(9-4)\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364453 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eba1f811-7b7d-56f6-8b49-4e7a4c7db81b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Moments of Inertia Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78e07db8-b668-530c-942c-fe79553c39e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{M}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364455} {:answer/id #uuid "e7f21f84-2745-572f-818d-0cd8eb07553f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364456} {:answer/id #uuid "7af9b035-fbd0-5066-9a00-8e00324ed2c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{M}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364457} {:answer/id #uuid "cd497303-f1f1-5811-9cae-4900033195e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364458} {:answer/id #uuid "6435fe47-e6d4-5682-945b-fa8cc369dfa3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364459}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd497303-f1f1-5811-9cae-4900033195e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364458} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0d8985c-4b56-598b-8540-4e41803354e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364454}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3def55ec-34fd-59d0-bba6-bc02cf16afcb" :question/math-academy-id "q-163242" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/d1a19303937f1d515ca820516e10a73974b00f83884b05abb58dee49c81207f4.png)\n\nConsider a plate of mass $M$ that occupies the region $D$ in the upper half plane bounded by the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16,$ shown above. Given that the mass is distributed uniformly across the plate, find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (r,\\theta ),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ in polar coordinates can be expressed as\n\n$$\nD={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4,\\,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe area of the plate $D$ is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2}=8\\pi .\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the mass is distributed uniformly, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{8\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{8\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{8\\pi }({\\int }_{0}^{4}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{8\\pi }({\\int }_{0}^{4}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{8\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}{|}_{0}^{4}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}\\theta +\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{8\\pi }\\cdot 64\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =4M.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364460 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eba1f811-7b7d-56f6-8b49-4e7a4c7db81b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Moments of Inertia Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c06625e2-9b78-5546-8cb9-2d47c794a31a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364462} {:answer/id #uuid "079e4805-87e4-56ba-97bc-e281254d86b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3M}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364463} {:answer/id #uuid "779f35a3-85fa-5fd1-be64-221cb452ca5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2M}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364464} {:answer/id #uuid "798ddd70-aee5-5615-9921-f6a0fec105ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364465} {:answer/id #uuid "8b457ab8-6be3-5636-b5b7-87afeeffc0e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{M}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364466}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c06625e2-9b78-5546-8cb9-2d47c794a31a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364462} :answer-field/id #uuid "e895b614-920e-57d2-9e01-0f2d045a8adf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364461}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fe5d4c58-2e38-5984-9eea-500802828713" :question/math-academy-id "q-163243" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/044386c438976bf17e2f51241659f167f7eedfb6509d0a60f3f70ba91457e71f.png)\n\nConsider a plate of mass $M$ that occupies the region $D$ in the first, second and third quadrants bounded by the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ shown above. Given that the mass is distributed uniformly across the plate, find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (r,\\theta ),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ in polar coordinates can be expressed as\n\n$$\nD={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe area of the plate $D$ is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{3}{4}\\pi {r}^{2}=3\\pi .\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the mass is distributed uniformly, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{3\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3\\pi }({\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3\\pi }({\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}{|}_{0}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}\\theta -\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ){|}_{0}^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3\\pi }\\cdot 4\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =M.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364467 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eba1f811-7b7d-56f6-8b49-4e7a4c7db81b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Moments of Inertia Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc6a82bb-4c72-5958-80ae-6f77875da8a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6M" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364469} {:answer/id #uuid "9bc6b333-2f93-5fb7-b376-4e22cd854b78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{28M}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364470} {:answer/id #uuid "39097f3b-e339-56c3-85ea-b1455eeb433e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16M}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364471} {:answer/id #uuid "b90b5a0a-3df5-5d31-a16f-a7ebaa95c490" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21M}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364472} {:answer/id #uuid "3658ca36-08d0-5e0f-bafc-20ab62d9bb6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14M}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364473}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3658ca36-08d0-5e0f-bafc-20ab62d9bb6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14M}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364473} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3a5a1e2-77d8-517c-8d3d-504629e93b24" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364468}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2eae4cf8-5a27-5bb2-bc3f-4dcf1775ac6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-38822" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069995/assets/3b603e45dd1e5fe3073cad13a5577389736c1103840f3f904e29e738b4f2fd2e.png)\n\nConsider a plate of mass $M$ that occupies the region $D$ in the upper half plane bounded by the polar circles $r=1,$ $r=2,$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above. The mass density at any point is proportional to the square of its distance from the origin. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the origin." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (r,\\theta ),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D,$ in polar coordinates, can be expressed as\n\n$$\nD={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nFrom the description, the mass density function can be written as $\\lambda (r,\\theta )=k{r}^{2},$ where $k$ is a constant of proportionality. We can find an expression for $k$ in terms of $M$ by integrating the density function over the plate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }k{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =k({\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =k\\cdot \\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}{|}_{1}^{2}\\cdot \\theta {|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =k\\cdot \\frac{15}{4}\\cdot \\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{15k\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\lambda (r,\\theta )=k{r}^{2}=\\frac{4M{r}^{2}}{15\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{15\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{5}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{15\\pi }({\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{5}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{15\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}{r}^{6}{|}_{1}^{2}\\cdot \\theta {|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{15\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}(64-1)\\cdot (\\pi -0) \\\\  & =\\frac{14M}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
